<% content_for :title, "AI agents for engineering teams | Norman Simplified" %>
<% content_for :description, "Agents for broken builds, alert triage and pull request review, with a clear answer on what the agent can reach and what leaves your network." %>
<% content_for :footer_note, "Norman Simplified is independent and is not affiliated with Anthropic." %>

<main class="ns-how">
  <section id="intro" class="ns-section ns-how__hero" aria-labelledby="eng-title">
    <div class="ns-container">
      <p class="sn-eyebrow">For software teams</p>
      <h1 id="eng-title" class="t-display ns-how__title">AI agents for engineering teams</h1>
      <p class="ns-lead">I build agents for three jobs: broken builds, alert triage and pull request review. Each one starts with a clear answer on what the agent can reach and what leaves your network.</p>
      <div class="ns-actions">
        <%= link_to "Request a call", studio_contact_path(interest: "pilot"), data: { controller: "analytics", action: "click->analytics#track", analytics_event_param: "cta_clicked", analytics_cta_param: "request_a_call", analytics_location_param: "hero", analytics_interest_param: "pilot" }, class: "sn-btn sn-btn--primary sn-btn--lg" %>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="security-owner" class="ns-section" aria-labelledby="security-owner-title">
    <div class="ns-container ns-editorial-grid">
      <header class="ns-section-heading">
        <h2 id="security-owner-title" class="t-heading">What the security owner will ask</h2>
      </header>
      <div class="ns-prose">
        <p><strong>What can it reach?</strong> Only what its sandbox and its network rules allow. The agent's shell is a real shell with the worker's permissions, so isolation comes from what the worker runs in.</p>
        <p><strong>Which credentials does it hold?</strong> Scoped to the job. A self-hosted service uses three: a server API key, an inference credential held by the server, and an environment key held by the workers.</p>
        <p><strong>Where is its history stored?</strong> With the vendor at the first two levels. With you at the third. Either way, the model still sees it.</p>
        <p><strong>What is sent to the model?</strong> Each model call sends the conversation so far, including tool inputs and results, to the inference provider. This is not offline.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="levels" class="ns-section" aria-labelledby="levels-title">
    <div class="ns-container">
      <header class="ns-section-heading">
        <h2 id="levels-title" class="t-heading">Three ways to host it</h2>
      </header>
      <div class="ns-table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-labelledby="levels-title">
        <table class="ns-table">
          <caption>Hosting levels for Claude Managed Agents, from least to most hosted by you</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Level</th>
              <th scope="col">What you host</th>
              <th scope="col">What reaches the vendor</th>
              <th scope="col">Access</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Vendor-hosted, scoped</th>
              <td>Nothing. You set network rules and credentials.</td>
              <td>Conversation, tool results, the sandbox's files and state, skills, memory stores</td>
              <td>Beta</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Your sandboxes</th>
              <td>The workers and sandboxes where tools run</td>
              <td>Conversation, tool results, skills, memory stores. Files and processes stay on your worker. Web search and web fetch still run on the vendor's side.</td>
              <td>Beta</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Your service and your sandboxes</th>
              <td>The server (<code>cma-server</code>), PostgreSQL 16 or later, workers and sandboxes. Session history is stored with you.</td>
              <td>Each model call sends the conversation so far, including tool inputs and results, to the inference provider: Anthropic by default, or Amazon Bedrock, Google Cloud, Microsoft Foundry, or your own gateway</td>
              <td>By request through an Anthropic account team</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="ns-prose">
        <p>These levels apply to any agent stack. Claude Managed Agents is the worked example.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="take-on" class="ns-section" aria-labelledby="take-on-title">
    <div class="ns-container ns-editorial-grid">
      <header class="ns-section-heading">
        <h2 id="take-on-title" class="t-heading">What you take on</h2>
      </header>
      <div class="ns-prose">
        <p>At the third level you run and secure the server, the database and the sandboxes, and you install each release.</p>
        <p>Session history is stored with you, but this is not offline. Each model call still sends the conversation so far, including tool inputs and results, to the inference provider.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="check" class="ns-section" aria-label="Vendor check">
    <div class="ns-container ns-editorial-grid">
      <div class="ns-prose">
        <p>Checked against vendor documentation on 14 November 2026.</p>
        <div class="ns-actions">
          <%= link_to "Start with a pilot", studio_contact_path(interest: "pilot"), data: { controller: "analytics", action: "click->analytics#track", analytics_event_param: "cta_clicked", analytics_cta_param: "request_a_call", analytics_location_param: "engineering_teams_pilot", analytics_interest_param: "pilot" }, class: "sn-btn sn-btn--primary sn-btn--lg" %>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</main>
